# Step by step: a JupyterLab "Workspace" menu that runs `ws-publish` and `ws-base`

Follow this top to bottom inside your Coder overlay workspace. Every block is meant to be copied and pasted.

The result is a **Workspace** button in the JupyterLab file browser toolbar (and a **Workspace** top menu). Each item runs a command behind the scene, so users never open a terminal:

| Menu item | Runs | What the user sees |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Workspace status | `ws-base status` | a dialog with base, latest, number of changes |
| Share a snapshot… | `ws-publish share` | confirm, then "Share: ok share ref=ws/alice/20260926-181500" |
| Promote… | `ws-publish promote` or `ws-publish promote -from-tag ws/…` | choose "my current changes" or "an existing share tag" |
| Update to latest… | `ws-base check latest`, then `ws-base use latest` (`--force` if the user accepts conflicts) | "No conflicts, update?" or the list of conflicting files with an "Update anyway" button |
| Go back to previous base… | `ws-base back` (`--force` if refused and the user insists) | confirm, then "rebased … -> …" |

How it fits together:

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  U[Browser: Workspace menu] -->|POST /ws-actions/run/share| S[Jupyter server extension\nws_actions/handlers.py]
  S -->|ws-publish share| P[ws-publish\nreads /data/overlay/upper + .base_commit_sha\npushes tag to GitLab]
  S -->|ws-base use latest| B[ws-base client]
  B -->|FIFO /tmp/ws-base/req| C[ws-base serve\nouter namespace: remounts the overlay]
```

JupyterLab is started by the Coder agent (through the Coder `jupyterlab` module), so it runs inside the overlay namespace like the terminal does: it sees the merged `/home/coder/workspace`, and `ws-base use` reaches the controller exactly as from a terminal.

Status: written for you to try; not yet run end to end in a Coder overlay workspace. The extension layout, build and packaging are the same as the validated `jupyter_keytab` lab; the new parts are the `ws-publish`/`ws-base` calls and the two dev scripts. Start with the dry run in [step 7.1](#71-first-run-in-dry-run-mode-ui-only-nothing-executed).

The approach for testing: keep the Coder `jupyterlab` module unchanged (it installs JupyterLab with `pipx` at workspace start), then link the extension source into that JupyterLab in "develop" mode. You edit, rebuild, refresh. When you are happy, you build a wheel and bake it into the image ([step 10](#10-when-you-are-happy-build-the-wheel-and-bake-it-into-the-image)).

