## 2. Add the module and the environment to the template

In the overlay template (`main.tf` from `OVERLAY-CODER-GUIDE.md` section 5), add the module next to `coder_agent.main`:

```hcl
module "jupyterlab" {
  count     = data.coder_workspace.me.start_count
  source    = "registry.coder.com/coder/jupyterlab/coder"
  version   = "<pin a version>"
  agent_id  = coder_agent.main.id
  subdomain = false
  config = jsonencode({
    ServerApp = {
      root_dir = "/home/coder/workspace"
      tornado_settings = {
        headers = {
          "Content-Security-Policy" = "frame-ancestors 'self' ${data.coder_workspace.me.access_url}"
        }
      }
    }
    FileCheckpoints = {
      root_dir = "/home/coder/.jupyter-checkpoints"
    }
  })
}
```

- `subdomain = false` makes it a path app: `https://<coder>/@<owner>/<workspace>/apps/jupyterlab/`. The app slug is `jupyterlab`, so the middle's link is `/go/jupyterlab`.
- `config` is written to `~/.jupyter/jupyter_server_config.json` by the module on **every** start (so don't hand-edit that file, it gets overwritten). `root_dir` opens the file browser on the overlay; `FileCheckpoints.root_dir` keeps `.ipynb_checkpoints` folders out of the overlay (otherwise they land in `upper/` and get published). The `Content-Security-Policy` line is what the module adds by itself when `config` is empty; keep it when you set `config`.

Add the variables `ws-publish` needs to the container `env` list in `local.pod_spec` (the Coder agent and everything it starts, including JupyterLab, inherit them):

```hcl
data "coder_external_auth" "gitlab" {
  id = "gitlab"
}

# in local.pod_spec.containers[0].env, next to CODER_AGENT_TOKEN:
        { name = "GITLAB_REMOTE", value = "https://gitlab.example.com/group/repo.git" },
        { name = "WS_USER", value = data.coder_workspace_owner.me.name },
        { name = "GITLAB_TOKEN", value = data.coder_external_auth.gitlab.access_token },
        { name = "SCRATCH_ROOT", value = "/data/overlay" },
```

- `SCRATCH_ROOT`: `ws-publish` defaults to `/scratch`; the overlay keeps `upper/` and `.base_commit_sha` in `/data/overlay`. (The extension also defaults it to `/data/overlay` if unset.)
- `GITLAB_TOKEN`: here from Coder external auth (the user connects GitLab once). The value is captured when the workspace starts; if your GitLab OAuth tokens are short-lived, restart the workspace to refresh it. A static test PAT in a Kubernetes Secret works too for a first test.

Push the template and move the workspace onto it:

```bash
coder templates push <template> -d .
coder update <workspace>
```

