## 6. Create the two helper scripts

### 6.1 `dev-setup.sh`: link the source into the module's JupyterLab

The module installed JupyterLab with `pipx`, so it lives in its own environment (`~/.local/share/pipx/venvs/jupyterlab` or similar; `pipx environment` tells you). The extension must be installed into **that** environment.

```bash
mkdir -p /home/coder/dev/scripts
cat > /home/coder/dev/scripts/dev-setup.sh <<'EOF'
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Link the ws_actions source into the JupyterLab environment the Coder jupyterlab module created
# with pipx, build the frontend, then restart JupyterLab so the server half loads.
# Safe to re-run. Survives workspace restarts (/home/coder is on a PVC); re-run only after
# a pipx upgrade/reinstall of jupyterlab.
set -euo pipefail

SRC=${WS_ACTIONS_SRC:-/home/coder/dev/ws_actions}
HERE=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)
VENV="$(pipx environment --value PIPX_LOCAL_VENVS)/jupyterlab"

[ -x "$VENV/bin/jupyter-lab" ] || { echo "JupyterLab not found in $VENV (has the module finished installing it?)"; exit 1; }
[ -f "$SRC/pyproject.toml" ] || { echo "extension source not found in $SRC"; exit 1; }
export PATH="$VENV/bin:$PATH"
cd "$SRC"

echo "==> editable install into $VENV"
pipx runpip jupyterlab install -q -e "$SRC"

echo "==> build the frontend"
jlpm install >/dev/null
jlpm build

echo "==> link the frontend and enable the server extension"
jupyter labextension develop . --overwrite
jupyter server extension enable --sys-prefix ws_actions

jupyter labextension list 2>&1 | grep -i ws-actions || { echo "frontend not registered"; exit 1; }
jupyter server extension list 2>&1 | grep -i ws_actions || { echo "server extension not registered"; exit 1; }

"$HERE/restart-jupyter.sh"
EOF
```

What each step does:

| Step | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `pipx runpip jupyterlab install -e` | Jupyter imports `ws_actions/*.py` straight from `/home/coder/dev/ws_actions` |
| `jlpm install` / `jlpm build` | compiles `src/*.ts` into `ws_actions/labextension/` |
| `jupyter labextension develop` | symlinks `ws_actions/labextension/` into the environment, so each rebuild is picked up |
| `jupyter server extension enable --sys-prefix` | turns the server half on in that environment |

### 6.2 `restart-jupyter.sh`: restart JupyterLab with the module's own arguments

Needed after Python changes. It reuses the arguments of the running JupyterLab, and adds `--ServerApp.root_dir` and `--FileCheckpoints.root_dir` only if they are missing (same values as the module `config` in step 2, as a safety net if you test without that `config`).

```bash
cat > /home/coder/dev/scripts/restart-jupyter.sh <<'EOF'
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Restart the JupyterLab started by the Coder jupyterlab module, with the same arguments,
# so Python changes (handlers.py) are picked up. Also points the file browser at the workspace
# and keeps .ipynb_checkpoints out of the overlay (they would end up in upper/ and get published).
set -euo pipefail

LOG=${JUPYTER_LOG:-/tmp/jupyterlab.log}
ROOT=${JUPYTER_ROOT:-$HOME/workspace}
CHECKPOINTS=${JUPYTER_CHECKPOINTS:-/home/coder/.jupyter-checkpoints}
PATTERN='jupyter-lab --no-browser'

ARGS=$(pgrep -af "$PATTERN" | head -1 | cut -d' ' -f2-) || true
[ -n "$ARGS" ] || { echo "JupyterLab is not running (start/restart the workspace first)"; exit 1; }

case "$ARGS" in *--ServerApp.root_dir=*) ;; *) ARGS="$ARGS --ServerApp.root_dir=$ROOT" ;; esac
case "$ARGS" in *--FileCheckpoints.root_dir=*) ;; *) ARGS="$ARGS --FileCheckpoints.root_dir=$CHECKPOINTS" ;; esac
mkdir -p "$CHECKPOINTS"

pkill -f "$PATTERN"
for _ in $(seq 1 30); do pgrep -f "$PATTERN" >/dev/null || break; sleep 0.5; done

# Start from $HOME, not inside the overlay: a cwd inside it would pin the old mount after an update.
cd "$HOME"
# shellcheck disable=SC2086  # ARGS is the module's argument list, split on spaces on purpose
setsid nohup $ARGS >"$LOG" 2>&1 </dev/null &

for _ in $(seq 1 60); do
  if grep -q 'ws_actions server extension loaded' "$LOG" 2>/dev/null; then
    echo "JupyterLab restarted; ws_actions loaded (log: $LOG)"
    exit 0
  fi
  sleep 1
done
echo "JupyterLab restarted but ws_actions was not seen in $LOG:"
tail -20 "$LOG"
exit 1
EOF

chmod +x /home/coder/dev/scripts/*.sh
```

Run it from a Coder terminal (or `coder ssh`), not from `kubectl exec`: only processes started under the agent see the overlay.

